/-!
# The ATLAS permutation model of the Tits group

This file defines a concrete finite model `TauCeti.TitsGroup` of the Tits group `²F₄(2)′`: the
subgroup of `Equiv.Perm (Fin 1600)` generated by the two permutations `TitsGroup.a` and
`TitsGroup.b` of the ATLAS representation `TF42G1-p1600B0` on the standard generators. The ATLAS
describes it as the primitive action of the Tits group on the `1600` cosets of a maximal subgroup
of shape `L₃(3):2`; that description is background and is not used here.

The model is the finite group with which the group presented by the ATLAS presentation
`TauCeti.TitsGroup.presentation` is compared. This file proves:

* the generators have orders `2` and `3` and their product has order `13`;
* the six relators of the presentation hold on `TitsGroup.a` and `TitsGroup.b`, so the presented
  group maps onto the model (`TitsGroup.presentationHom`, `TitsGroup.range_presentationHom`,
  `TitsGroup.exists_surjective_titsGroup`), and both groups are nontrivial;
* the model is transitive on the `1600` points and perfect.

Neither the order of the model nor its primitivity or simplicity is asserted here, and the
surjection is not shown to be injective.

## Conventions

The tables are the forward images in the ATLAS files `TF42G1-p1600B0.m1` and
`TF42G1-p1600B0.m2`, with the file's points `1, …, 1600` renumbered `0, …, 1599`. Products follow
Mathlib's convention `(σ * τ) x = σ (τ x)`, and it is in this convention that the relators are
evaluated: the presentation generator of index `0` goes to `TitsGroup.a` and the one of index `1`
to `TitsGroup.b`. (The source composes permutations from left to right. Its relators also hold in
that convention, which amounts to sending the two generators to the inverses of the two
permutations; the range is the same subgroup.)

Transitivity is certified by a descent: a table `rank` of natural numbers such that every point
other than `0` is mapped by `TitsGroup.a` or by `TitsGroup.b` to a point of smaller rank
(`MulAction.isPretransitive_closure_of_forall_exists_lt`). The rank of a point is the length of a
shortest word in the two generators taking it to `0`; only the kernel-checked inequalities are
used.

The lift and range arguments use `GroupPresentation.homEquiv` and
`GroupPresentation.exists_surjective_of_closure_range_eq`.

## Main declarations

* `TauCeti.TitsGroup`: the permutation model of the Tits group on `1600` points, generated by
  `TauCeti.TitsGroup.a` and `TauCeti.TitsGroup.b`.
* `TauCeti.TitsGroup.orderOf_a`, `TauCeti.TitsGroup.orderOf_b`,
  `TauCeti.TitsGroup.orderOf_a_mul_b`: the orders `2`, `3` and `13`.
* `TauCeti.TitsGroup.lift_presentationImage_toFreeGroup_eq_one`: the six relators hold.
* `TauCeti.TitsGroup.presentationHom`, `TauCeti.TitsGroup.range_presentationHom`,
  `TauCeti.TitsGroup.exists_surjective_titsGroup`: the presented group maps onto the model.
* `TauCeti.TitsGroup.instIsPretransitive`: the model is transitive on the `1600` points.
* `TauCeti.isPerfect_titsGroup`: the model is perfect.

## References

* R. A. Wilson, R. A. Parker, J. N. Bray et al., *ATLAS of Finite Group Representations*,
  version 3, representation `TF42G1-p1600B0` of the Tits group,
  <https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/v3/permrep/TF42G1-p1600B0>. The image data are from
  <https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/exc/TF42/mtx/TF42G1-p1600B0.m1> (SHA-256
  `69bc8b66eb935383d0b05fc384a46138ad52b31b14dfd4fa8978734c6fd6d35c`) and
  <https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/exc/TF42/mtx/TF42G1-p1600B0.m2> (SHA-256
  `2acc14ac76d3cc48e9de69294e496f3115e812fc10b251037262257cf3dc4f0f`).
-/

